@* Kept, rather than the stopgap it went in as.

   The starfield is correct and useless at the same time: stars that far away genuinely don't shift
   as you move, which means nothing on screen moves when the camera does and flying around looks
   like standing still. A grid pinned to world coordinates - Parallax 1, the default, so it moves
   and turns and scales exactly with everything else - was the crudest thing that would restore the
   sense of motion, and turned out to be worth having on its own merits. Not set in stone; little
   here is. *@
<defs>
    <pattern id="sporbits-grid-minor" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" width="5" height="5">
        <path class="grid-minor" d="M 5 0 L 0 0 0 5" />
    </pattern>

    <pattern id="sporbits-grid-major" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" width="20" height="20">
        <path class="grid-major" d="M 20 0 L 0 0 0 20" />
    </pattern>
</defs>

@* Wrap on the major spacing, which the minor one divides into, so one value bounds both. *@
<CameraLayer Camera="Camera" Wrap="20">
    <rect x="-500" y="-500" width="1000" height="1000" fill="url(#sporbits-grid-minor)" />
    <rect x="-500" y="-500" width="1000" height="1000" fill="url(#sporbits-grid-major)" />
</CameraLayer>

@code {
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public Camera Camera { get; set; } = default!;
}
